"# Appendix M — Grid flowers + pocket motion

A \"flower\" in Motoi is a grid-anchored named actor. You spawn one
at a cell, tell it where to go, then either advance it a step per
frame or ask if it has settled. Nothing about the flower says
\"petals\" — you draw whatever sprite you like at its current cell.
The flower is just a state record with a name.

The eight verbs form a small state machine:

    (grid/flower-spawn!    'rose 0 0)      ; put a flower named 'rose at (0,0)
    (grid/flower-go-to!    'rose 5 5)      ; set its target
    (grid/flower-move-to!  'rose)          ; advance one Chebyshev step
    (grid/flower-pos       'rose)          ; => (1 1) after one step
    (grid/flower-settled?  'rose)          ; => #f until (c,r) == target
    (grid/flower-state!    'rose 'bloom)   ; set an arbitrary state symbol
    (grid/flower-list)                     ; => (\"rose\")
    (grid/flower-clear!)                   ; drop every flower

The default `st` on spawn is `'idle`. Once you call `flower-go-to!`
it becomes `'walking`. When `move-to!` reaches the target it flips
back to `'idle` and `settled?` returns `#t`. You can drive your own
state values (`'bloom` `'wilted` `'seed`) via `flower-state!` and
inspect them each frame with `(grid/flower-state! 'rose)` (no second
arg reads the current value).

Chebyshev steps mean diagonals are one step, same as horizontal or
vertical — kids expect that from Chess kings and Sokoban actors.

## `motion/pocket` — settle at a pocket target

The pocket verb records where an entity should end up. Callers wire
the actual interpolation via `motion/glide!` or `motion/arc`; pocket
just marks the intent so downstream code can query the target
without re-parsing every tween.

    (motion/pocket 'card-42 100 200)
    ; => (motion/pocket \"card-42\" 100 200)

The entity's meta record picks up `pocket: [100, 200]` for later
lookup. Kids use this when three cards must all settle at the same
row-column so a shuffle animation ends visibly-neatly.

Every verb above runs through `bin/motoi eval` today.
")
